
== Instruction Set

((DUSTY-12)) processing core provides 31 basic instructions. These cover things like math, basic stack manipulations, comparisons, conditional and unconditional jumps, memory access, data and power control.

Initially, all instructions are locked in the Code Selector section of Core Monitor. Only number placement is available there.

Although the DUSTY-12 processing core installed on the probe supports the full range of instructions, it is considered unwise to open access to all of them at once.

Common deep-space probe operation practices call for a gradual unlock of features as you gain operational experience. Tech support is going to unlock new instructions as the need arises - you'll be notified about that by email.

```
NOP ( -- ) - skip the operation and do nothing this cycle
DROP (x -- ) - drop the top value on the stack
DUP (x -- x x) - duplicate the top value on the stack
SWAP (x y -- y x) - swap top two values on stack
ROT (x y z -- y z x) - rotate top three values on stack
POKE (x @ -- ) - set the memory cell with the specified value
PEEK (@ -- x) - push on stack the value at the provided address
ADD (x y -- [x+y]) - add two values at the top of the data stack
SUB (x y -- [x-y]) - subtract the top number from the previous one
MUL (x y -- [x*y]) - multiply two values
DIV (x y -- [x/y]) - divide by the top value
MOD (x y -- [x%y]) - get the remainder from the division
NEG (x -- -x) - negate the top data stack value
EQ (x y -- 1/0) - compare if the top two values are equal
NEQ (x y -- 0/1) - check if the top two values are not equal
LT (x y -- 1/0) - check if the top value is less than the next
LTE (x y -- 1/0) - check on less or equal
GT (x y -- 1/0) - check if the top value is greater than the next
GTE (x y -- 1/0) - check on greater or equal
NOT (x -- 1/0) - logical NOT for the top value on the data stack
JMP (@ -- ) - unconditional jump to the specified address
JNZ (x @ -- ) - jump only if the second value is not zero
OBUS (x1 -- ) - open data line to the specified instrument
CBUS (x1 -- ) - close the data line to the specified instrument
OPOW (x1 -- ) - open the power line to the specified instrument
CPOW (x1 -- ) - close the power line to the specified instrument
IN (n -- ) - read the specified #n i/o line
OUT (d n  -- ) - send the value d to #n i/o line
WAIT (n -- ) - wait for n seconds
HALT ( -- ) - halt execution
RST ((... -- clear memory, stacks and registers)) - reset the VM

```

== Lines and Ports

Lines and ports are numbered and associated with on-board instruments. Every DUSTY-12 data/power line and port has a number. Lines and ports with the same number are associated with the same device for convenience.

For example, the tape recorder is connected to socket #5, so it can be controlled with engineering telemetry data line #5, power line #5, and the I/O port #5.

The following block lists all the lines/ports available in the ANGEL-F5 probe with the associated on-board devices:

```
#0: gyroscope
#1: RTG
#2: battery
#3: spaceframe
#4: antenna
#5: tapeRecorder
#6: wideAngleCamera
#7: stackInspector
```





Use this reference table to find the line number to use with OBUS/CBUS and OPOW/CPOW instructions as well as IN/OUT commands.

For example, if I want to receive engineering telemetry from the high-gain antenna (#4), I can run:

```
04
OBUS
```
I place the value 0x04 on the data stack as a parameter and execute the OBUS instruction.

Or when I want to power on the same high-gain antenna, I would use a similar sequence, but with the OPOW instruction:

```
04
OPOW
```

